 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: after a close, the remaining tabs are renumbered in order (`t1,t2,t3,…`) and `next` is reset to
+  `tabs.length + 1`, so keys are always contiguous, including after a later open.
+- Forbidden: leaving gaps in the key sequence after a close.
+- Reason: maintainer override of §stable-tab-keys (2026-10-22). The cost recorded there is accepted: closing a tab
+  remounts every tab to the right of it, which drops their scroll position and unsaved form input, and the same
+  key can name different tabs over time in URL state (`?tabs=`).
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "open after close stays contiguous")
+
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others (superseded)
+- Superseded: 2026-10-22 by §contiguous-tab-keys (maintainer override)
 - Since: 2026-09-02
 - Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
 - Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
 - Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
   and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` (the "close keeps keys" test, since replaced by "close renumbers keys")
